<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imo River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imo River is a river of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, about 240 km long, that flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a> to the Atlantic near Opobo. In the 19th century it was a key route for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> interior to the coast. In 1870 Jaja founded Opobo near its mouth, cutting off Bonny from the interior markets. Imo State, created in 1976, is named after it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imo-river.jpg" alt="Imo River" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emmanuel Obiajulu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imo_river_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
